# Code conventions

These hold whatever the language is. Anything specific to a stack lives in its own file, so
that C# work never has React conventions attached to it:

| Stack                              | File                                                         |
| ---------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |
| TypeScript, React, Node, CSS       | [code-conventions-ts.mdc](code-conventions-ts.mdc)           |

Cursor attaches the matching file by its globs. Claude Code and Copilot have no equivalent,
so read this file plus the one for the stack you detected — and if the stack has no file
yet, follow the conventions already present in the repository.

## Naming

- Named constants replace magic numbers and repeated literals. Declare them at the
  narrowest scope that serves every use.
- Names say what the thing is, not what type it is or how it is implemented.

## Structure

- Organise by feature rather than by layer, with shared logic in `common/`,
  application-wide configuration in `config/`, and global constants in `constants/`.
- Keep units small and single-purpose. When something grows past a single responsibility,
  split it rather than adding a flag to it.
- Extend existing shared code instead of building a parallel version. When a shared
  component or helper nearly fits, add the missing capability there.

## Logging

- Log through the project's own logger — `logDebug` for diagnostics, `logError` for
  failures — so nothing noisy survives into production. Never write diagnostics straight to
  stdout or stderr from shipped code.

## Linting

- Code must pass the linter and the type checker as written. Never disable, suppress,
  reconfigure or bypass a rule to make a change pass. A rule that fires is information.

## Comments and clarity

- Comments explain a constraint the code cannot express. They do not narrate what the next
  line does, or why a change was made.
- Keep it simple: the straightforward version a reader understands on first pass beats the
  clever one.
